[[:db/cas 17592186421990 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of cured patients within a week.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=50$ patients in the sample\n\n- the probability that the new drug has cured a patient within a week is $p=64%=0.64$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(50,0.64).$ \n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{np(1-p)},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the standard deviation of the number of cured patients within a week is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{np(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(50)(0.64)(1-0.64)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(50)(0.64)(0.36)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11.52} \\\\  & ≈3.394\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of cured patients within a week.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=50$ patients in the sample\n\n- the probability that the new drug has cured a patient within a week is $p=64\\%=0.64$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(50,0.64).$ \n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{np(1-p)},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the standard deviation of the number of cured patients within a week is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{np(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(50)(0.64)(1-0.64)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(50)(0.64)(0.36)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11.52} \\\\  & ≈3.394\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186421991 :question/problem "An archer practices shooting $16$ arrows at a target. The archer has a $25%$ chance of hitting the bull's eye. What is the variance in the number of arrows that hit the bull's eye?" "An archer practices shooting $16$ arrows at a target. The archer has a $25\\%$ chance of hitting the bull's eye. What is the variance in the number of arrows that hit the bull's eye?"] [:db/cas 17592186421991 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of arrows that hit the bull's eye.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=16$ shots\n\n- the probability of hitting the bull's eye for each arrow is $p=25%=0.25$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(16,0.25).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\n So, the variance of the number of arrows hitting the bull's eye is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =(16)(0.25)(1-0.25) \\\\  & =(4)(0.75) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of arrows that hit the bull's eye.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=16$ shots\n\n- the probability of hitting the bull's eye for each arrow is $p=25\\%=0.25$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(16,0.25).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\n So, the variance of the number of arrows hitting the bull's eye is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =(16)(0.25)(1-0.25) \\\\  & =(4)(0.75) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186423858 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=3$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | ${x}_{1}$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we orthogonalize ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process to get the corresponding orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nLet ${c}_{1}={b}_{1},$ then:\n\n|  |  | $-4$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} |  |  |\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}$\n              </td>\n<td align=