u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n$0$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"] [:db/cas 17592186424807 :question/problem "If a circle represents a whole, which model shows $210%?$" "If a circle represents a whole, which model shows $210\\%?$"] [:db/cas 17592186424807 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that $210%$ is the same as $\\frac{210}{100}.$\n\nWe now need to simplify this fraction. Dividing the numerator and denominator by $10,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{210}{100}=\\frac{210÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{21}{10}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write $\\frac{21}{10}$ as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{21}{10}=2\\,\\text{R}\\,1=2\\,\\frac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nThe model that shows $2\\,\\frac{1}{10}$ is:\n\n![](images/b9/b901819e40d67559b5b8f6d137ab5595531801f5529f778be34c768b2dc40c3f.png)" "First, recall that $210\\%$ is the same as $\\frac{210}{100}.$\n\nWe now need to simplify this fraction. Dividing the numerator and denominator by $10,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{210}{100}=\\frac{210÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{21}{10}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write $\\frac{21}{10}$ as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{21}{10}=2\\,\\text{R}\\,1=2\\,\\frac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nThe model that shows $2\\,\\frac{1}{10}$ is:\n\n![](images/b9/b901819e40d67559b5b8f6d137ab5595531801f5529f778be34c768b2dc40c3f.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186424830 :question/problem "Mina and Sora have the same number of songs in a playlist. A total of $58%$ of Mina's songs are from the 2000s, while $\\frac{13}{20}$ of Sora's songs are from the 2000s. Select the options that make the statements below true.\n\n$\\,$\nA total of \n{{field-1}}\nof Sora's songs are from the 2000s. \n\n$\\,$\nTherefore, she has \n{{field-2}}\nsongs from the 2000s compared to Mina." "Mina and Sora have the same number of songs in a playlist. A total of $58\\%$ of Mina's songs are from the 2000s, while $\\frac{13}{20}$ of Sora's songs are from the 2000s. Select the options that make the statements below true.\n\n$\\,$\nA total of \n{{field-1}}\nof Sora's songs are from the 2000s. \n\n$\\,$\nTherefore, she has \n{{field-2}}\nsongs from the 2000s compared to Mina."] [:db/cas 17592186424830 :question/worked-solution "We need to compare $\\frac{13}{20}$ with $58%.$ We can do this by writing $\\frac{13}{20}$ as a percent.\n\nFirst, we write $\\frac{13}{20}$ as an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{13}{20}=\\frac{13\\times 5}{20\\times 5}=\\frac{65}{100}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{65}{100}=65%\n$$\n\nSince $65%>58%,$ we have the following:\n\n- A total of $65%$ of Sora's songs are from the 2000s.\n\n- Therefore, she has $\\text{more}$ songs from the 2000s compared to Mina." "We need to compare $\\frac{13}{20}$ with $58\\%.$ We can do this by writing $\\frac{13}{20}$ as a percent.\n\nFirst, we write $\\frac{13}{20}$ as an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{13}{20}=\\frac{13\\times 5}{20\\times 5}=\\frac{65}{100}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{65}{100}=65\\%\n$$\n\nSince $65\\%>58\\%,$ we have the following:\n\n- A total of $65\\%$ of Sora's songs are from the 2000s.\n\n- Therefore, she has $\\text{more}$ songs from the 2000s compared to Mina."] [:db/cas 17592186430359 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{3}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}$ |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | ${x}_{2}$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we orthogonalize ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process to get the corresponding orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nLet ${c}_{1}={b}_{1},$ then:\n\n|  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${c}_{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | $1$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} |  |  |\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"