{{field-1}},\nwhich is \n{{field-2}}\n$\\frac{17}{40}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186434387 :question/worked-solution "To convert the percent to a fraction, we first write $45%$ as a fraction with a denominator $100:$\n\n$$\n45%=\\frac{45}{100}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{45}{100}=\\frac{45÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{9}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can write this with a denominator of $40$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{20}=\\frac{9\\times 2}{20\\times 2}=\\frac{18}{40}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{18}{40}>\\frac{17}{40}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $45%$ is equivalent to $\\frac{18}{40},$ \nwhich is $\\text{greater than}$ $\\frac{17}{40}.$" "To convert the percent to a fraction, we first write $45\\%$ as a fraction with a denominator $100:$\n\n$$\n45\\%=\\frac{45}{100}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{45}{100}=\\frac{45÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{9}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can write this with a denominator of $40$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{20}=\\frac{9\\times 2}{20\\times 2}=\\frac{18}{40}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{18}{40}>\\frac{17}{40}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $45\\%$ is equivalent to $\\frac{18}{40},$ \nwhich is $\\text{greater than}$ $\\frac{17}{40}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186434388 :question/problem "Sana and Talia are students. In the previous semester, the decimal proportion of Sana’s homework assignments that were turned in early was $0.4,$ while $45%$ of Talia’s homework assignments were turned in early. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Sana and Talia are students. In the previous semester, the decimal proportion of Sana’s homework assignments that were turned in early was $0.4,$ while $45\\%$ of Talia’s homework assignments were turned in early. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186434388 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “Less than half of Sana's homework assignments were turned in early” is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n0.4<0.5,\n$$\n\nless than half of Sana's homework assignments were turned in early.\n\n- Statement “More than half of Talia's homework assignments were turned in early” is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n45%<50%.\n$$\n\nSo, less than half of Talia's homework assignments were turned in early.\n\n- Statement “Sana had a greater percentage of homework assignments turned in early compared to Talia” is false. We can compare $0.4$ and $45%$ by converting the decimal into a percent. \n\nTo do this, we multiply it by $100:$\n\n$$\n0.4\\times 100=40%\n$$\n\nSince  $40%<45%,$ Sana did not have a greater percentage of homework assignments turned in early compared to Talia.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “Less than half of Sana's homework assignments were turned in early” only." "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “Less than half of Sana's homework assignments were turned in early” is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n0.4<0.5,\n$$\n\nless than half of Sana's homework assignments were turned in early.\n\n- Statement “More than half of Talia's homework assignments were turned in early” is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n45\\%<50\\%.\n$$\n\nSo, less than half of Talia's homework assignments were turned in early.\n\n- Statement “Sana had a greater percentage of homework assignments turned in early compared to Talia” is false. We can compare $0.4$ and $45\\%$ by converting the decimal into a percent. \n\nTo do this, we multiply it by $100:$\n\n$$\n0.4\\times 100=40\\%\n$$\n\nSince  $40\\%<45\\%,$ Sana did not have a greater percentage of homework assignments turned in early compared to Talia.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “Less than half of Sana's homework assignments were turned in early” only."] [:db/cas 17592186435596 :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{17}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{17},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{17}{x}_{i}=8,\\,{\\prod }_{i=1}^{17}({x}_{i})!=4.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$\n\n$$" "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{17}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{17},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{17}{x}_{i}=8,\\,{\\prod }_{i=1}^{17}({x}_{i})!=4.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186435653 :question/problem "A quality‑check sensor detects a flaw with probability $40%.$ If $5$ items are inspected, rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that at most $2$ of them contain a flaw?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." "A quality‑check sensor detects a flaw with probability $40\\%.$ If $5$ items are inspected, rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that at most $2$ of them contain a flaw?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186435653 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of flawed items detected.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ items.\n\n- The probability of a flaw is $p=40%=0.4.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.4).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.4)^{x}(1-0.4)^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.4)^{x}(0.6)^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at most $2$ of the $5$ items contain flaws. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0})(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})(0.4)^{2}(0.6)^{3} \\\\  & =1(0.6)^{5}+5(0.4)(0.6)^{4}+10(0.4)^{2}(0.6)^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.683,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of flawed items detected.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ items.\n\n- The probability of a flaw is $p=40\\%=0.4.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.4).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.4)^{x}(1-0.4)^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.4)^{x}(0.6)^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at most $2$ of the $5$ items contain flaws. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0})(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})(0.4)^{2}(0.6)^{3} \\\\  & =1(0.6)^{5}+5(0.4)(0.6)^{4}+10(0.4)^{2}(0.6)^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.683,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186435654 :question/problem "An archer practices firing an arrow at a target $20$ times. If the archer has a $20%$ chance of hitting the bullseye, what is the probability that the archer hits the bullseye at least $2$ times? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "An archer practices firing an arrow at a target $20$ times. If the archer has a $20\\%$ chance of hitting the bullseye, what is the probability that the archer hits the bullseye at least $2$ times? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186435654 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the arrow hits the bullseye. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=20$ trials\n\n- the probability of hitting the bull's eye is $p=20%=0.2$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(20,0.2).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{x})(0.2)^{x}(1-0.2)^{20-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{20-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the archer hits the bullseye at least $2$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=20)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{0})(0.2)^{0}(0.8)^{20-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{1})(0.2)^{1}(0.8)^{20-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.8)^{20}+20(0.2)(0.8)^{19}] \\\\  & =1-0.069\\,175 \\\\  & =0.931\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the arrow hits the bullseye. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=20$ trials\n\n- the probability of hitting the bull's eye is $p=20\\%=0.2$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(20,0.2).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{x})(0.2)^{x}(1-0.2)^{20-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{20-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the archer hits the bullseye at least $2$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=20)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{0})(0.2)^{0}(0.8)^{20-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{1})(0.2)^{1}(0.8)^{20-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.8)^{20}+20(0.2)(0.8)^{19}] \\\\  & =1-0.069\\,175 \\\\  & =0.931\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186435655 :question/problem "An unfair coin has a $15%$ chance of landing on heads. The coin is tossed $10$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of getting heads at least $2$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." "An unfair coin has a $15\\%$ chance of landing on heads. The coin is tossed $10$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of getting heads at least $2$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186435655 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the coin landed on heads.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=10$ tosses.\n\n- The probability of landing on heads is $p=15%=0.15.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.15).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.15)^{x}(1-0.15)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.15)^{x}(0.85)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting heads at least $2$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=10)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0})(0.15)^{0}(0.85)^{10-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1})(0.15)^{1}(0.85)^{10-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.85)^{10}+10(0.15)(0.85)^{9}] \\\\  & ≈1-0.544\\,300 \\\\  & ≈0.456,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the coin landed on heads.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=10$ tosses.\n\n- The probability of landing on heads is $p=15\\%=0.15.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.15).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.15)^{x}(1-0.15)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.15)^{x}(0.85)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting heads at least $2$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=10)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0})(0.15)^{0}(0.85)^{10-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1})(0.15)^{1}(0.85)^{10-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.85)^{10}+10(0.15)(0.85)^{9}] \\\\  & ≈1-0.544\\,300 \\\\  & ≈0.456,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186435656 :question/problem "In a coffee shop, $28%$ of the customers order their coffee to go. If $12$ customers from the coffee shop are randomly selected, what is the probability that $3$ or less will order their coffee to go? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "In a coffee shop, $28\\%$ of the customers order their coffee to go. If $12$ customers from the coffee shop are randomly selected, what is the probability that $3$ or less will order their coffee to go? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186435656 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of customers that order their coffee to go.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ customers\n\n- the probability that each customer orders the coffee to go is $p=28%=0.28$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(12,0.28).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x}){(0.28)}^{x}{(1-0.28)}^{12-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x}){(0.28)}^{x}{(0.72)}^{12-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting a \"success\" (i.e., the customer orders the coffee to go) on at most $3$ trials. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{0}){(0.28)}^{0}{(0.72)}^{12}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{1}){(0.28)}^{1}{(0.72)}^{11}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{2}){(0.28)}^{2}{(0.72)}^{10}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{3}){(0.28)}^{3}{(0.72)}^{9} \\\\  & ={(0.28)}^{0}{(0.72)}^{12}+12{(0.28)}^{1}{(0.72)}^{11}+66{(0.28)}^{2}{(0.72)}^{10}+220{(0.28)}^{3}{(0.72)}^{9} \\\\  & =0.555\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of customers that order their coffee to go.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ customers\n\n- the probability that each customer orders the coffee to go is $p=28\\%=0.28$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(12,0.28).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x}){(0.28)}^{x}{(1-0.28)}^{12-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x}){(0.28)}^{x}{(0.72)}^{12-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting a \"success\" (i.e., the customer orders the coffee to go) on at most $3$ trials. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{0}){(0.28)}^{0}{(0.72)}^{12}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{1}){(0.28)}^{1}{(0.72)}^{11}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{2}){(0.28)}^{2}{(0.72)}^{10}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{3}){(0.28)}^{3}{(0.72)}^{9} \\\\  & ={(0.28)}^{0}{(0.72)}^{12}+12{(0.28)}^{1}{(0.72)}^{11}+66{(0.28)}^{2}{(0.72)}^{10}+220{(0.28)}^{3}{(0.72)}^{9} \\\\  & =0.555\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186436222 :question/problem "In a toy store, a total of $80$ green and red marbles are distributed between three boxes, $A,B,$ and $C.$ Box $A$ contains $16$ red marbles, while box $B$ contains $14$ red marbles. If $50%$ of all the marbles are red, what is the probability that a marble chosen at random is a red marble from box $C?$" "In a toy store, a total of $80$ green and red marbles are distributed between three boxes, $A,B,$ and $C.$ Box $A$ contains $16$ red marbles, while box $B$ contains $14$ red marbles. If $50\\%$ of all the marbles are red, what is the probability that a marble chosen at random is a red marble from box $C?$"] [:db/cas 17592186436222 :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that the selected marble is red, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected marble is from box $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected marble is from box $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected marble is from box $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nRecall that the law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(R)=P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find $P(R\\cap C):$\n\n- To calculate $P(R\\cap A),$ we take the number of red marbles in box $A$ $(16)$ and divide by the total number of marbles $(80).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(R\\cap A)=\\frac{16}{80}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(R\\cap B),$ we take the number of red marbles in box $B$ $(14)$ and divide by the total number of marbles $(80).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(R\\cap B)=\\frac{14}{80}.\n$$\n\n- Also, we're given that\n\n$$\nP(R)=50%=\\frac{40}{80}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(R\\cap C),$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C) \\\\ \\frac{40}{80} & =\\frac{16}{80}+\\frac{14}{80}+P(R\\cap C) \\\\ P(R\\cap C) & =\\frac{40}{80}-\\frac{16}{80}-\\frac{14}{80} \\\\ P(R\\cap C) & =\\frac{10}{80} \\\\ P(R\\cap C) & =12.5%\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $R$ be the event that the selected marble is red, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected marble is from box $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected marble is from box $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected marble is from box $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nRecall that the law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(R)=P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find $P(R\\cap C):$\n\n- To calculate $P(R\\cap A),$ we take the number of red marbles in box $A$ $(16)$ and divide by the total number of marbles $(80).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(R\\cap A)=\\frac{16}{80}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(R\\cap B),$ we take the number of red marbles in box $B$ $(14)$ and divide by the total number of marbles $(80).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(R\\cap B)=\\frac{14}{80}.\n$$\n\n- Also, we're given that\n\n$$\nP(R)=50\\%=\\frac{40}{80}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(R\\cap C),$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C) \\\\ \\frac{40}{80} & =\\frac{16}{80}+\\frac{14}{80}+P(R\\cap C) \\\\ P(R\\cap C) & =\\frac{40}{80}-\\frac{16}{80}-\\frac{14}{80} \\\\ P(R\\cap C) & =\\frac{10}{80} \\\\ P(R\\cap C) & =12.5\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186436223 :question/problem "A company manufactures NY Yankees caps at three different plants. Plant $A$ produces $36%$ of the caps, of which $25%$ are white, Plant $B$ produces $34%$ of the caps, of which  $40%$ are white, and Plant $C$ produces the remaining $30%$ of all the caps. If the probability that a randomly selected cap from any of the three plants is white is $0.46,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected cap from plant $C$ is white?" "A company manufactures NY Yankees caps at three different plants. Plant $A$ produces $36\\%$ of the caps, of which $25\\%$ are white, Plant $B$ produces $34\\%$ of the caps, of which  $40\\%$ are white, and Plant $C$ produces the remaining $30\\%$ of all the caps. If the probability that a randomly selected cap from any of the three plants is white is $0.46,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected cap from plant $C$ is white?"] [:db/cas 17592186436223 :question/worked-solution "Let $W$ be the event that a randomly selected cap is white, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the cap is from plant $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the cap is from plant $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the cap is from plant $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected cap from any of the three plants is white, we can use the law of total probability:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W) & =P(W\\cap A)+P(W\\cap B)+P(W\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|B)P(B)+P(W|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since plant $A$ produces $36%$ of all the caps, plant $B$ produces $34%$ of all the caps, and plant $C$ produces $30%$ of all the caps, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.36,\\,P(B)=0.34,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $25%$ of the caps from plant $A$ and $40%$ of the caps from plant $B$ are white, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|A)=0.25,\\,P(W|B)=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected cap from any of the three plants is white is $0.46,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(W)=0.46.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(W|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W) & =P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|B)P(B)+P(W|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.46 & =(0.25)(0.36)+(0.4)(0.34)+P(W|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.46 & =0.09+0.136+0.3P(W|C) \\\\ 0.3P(W|C) & =0.234 \\\\ P(W|C) & =0.78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected cap from plant $C$ is white is $0.78.$" "Let $W$ be the event that a randomly selected cap is white, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the cap is from plant $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the cap is from plant $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the cap is from plant $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected cap from any of the three plants is white, we can use the law of total probability:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W) & =P(W\\cap A)+P(W\\cap B)+P(W\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|B)P(B)+P(W|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since plant $A$ produces $36\\%$ of all the caps, plant $B$ produces $34\\%$ of all the caps, and plant $C$ produces $30\\%$ of all the caps, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.36,\\,P(B)=0.34,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $25\\%$ of the caps from plant $A$ and $40\\%$ of the caps from plant $B$ are white, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|A)=0.25,\\,P(W|B)=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected cap from any of the three plants is white is $0.46,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(W)=0.46.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(W|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W) & =P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|B)P(B)+P(W|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.46 & =(0.25)(0.36)+(0.4)(0.34)+P(W|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.46 & =0.09+0.136+0.3P(W|C) \\\\ 0.3P(W|C) & =0.234 \\\\ P(W|C) & =0.78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected cap from plant $C$ is white is $0.78.$"] [:db/cas 17592186436224 :question/problem "A company manufactures electronic components. Of the component population, $45%$ are produced by machine $A,$  $25%$ by machine $B,$ and $30%$ by machine $C.$ If $5%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ and $3%$ of those produced by machine $B$ are defective, and the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is $0.042,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $C$ is defective?" "A company manufactures electronic components. Of the component population, $45\\%$ are produced by machine $A,$  $25\\%$ by machine $B,$ and $30\\%$ by machine $C.$ If $5\\%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ and $3\\%$ of those produced by machine $B$ are defective, and the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is $0.042,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $C$ is defective?"] [:db/cas 17592186436224 :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected component is defective, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation. \n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $45%$ of the components, machine $B$ produces $25%$ of the components, and machine $C$ produces $30%$ of the components, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.45,\\,P(B)=0.25,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $5%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ and $3%$ of the components produced by machine $B$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.05,\\,P(D|B)=0.03\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is $0.042,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.042.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.042 & =(0.05)(0.45)+(0.03)(0.25)+P(D|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.042 & =0.0225+0.0075+0.3P(D|C) \\\\ 0.3P(D|C) & =0.012 \\\\ P(D|C) & =0.04\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $C$ is defective is $0.04.$" "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected component is defective, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation. \n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $45\\%$ of the components, machine $B$ produces $25\\%$ of the components, and machine $C$ produces $30\\%$ of the components, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.45,\\,P(B)=0.25,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $5\\%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ and $3\\%$ of the components produced by machine $B$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.05,\\,P(D|B)=0.03\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is $0.042,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.042.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.042 & =(0.05)(0.45)+(0.03)(0.25)+P(D|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.042 & =0.0225+0.0075+0.3P(D|C) \\\\ 0.3P(D|C) & =0.012 \\\\ P(D|C) & =0.04\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $C$ is defective is $0.04.$"] [:db/cas 17592186436225 :question/problem "A company produces replacement batteries for a particular cell phone model at three locations. Location $A$ produces $30%$ of the batteries, of which $3%$ are defective. Location $B$ produces $38%$ of the batteries, of which $5%$ are defective. And location $C$  produces $32%$ of the batteries, of which also $5%$ are defective. What is the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective?" "A company produces replacement batteries for a particular cell phone model at three locations. Location $A$ produces $30\\%$ of the batteries, of which $3\\%$ are defective. Location $B$ produces $38\\%$ of the batteries, of which $5\\%$ are defective. And location $C$  produces $32\\%$ of the batteries, of which also $5\\%$ are defective. What is the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective?"] [:db/cas 17592186436225 :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that the battery is defective, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the battery was produced at location $A,$\n\n- let $B$  be the event that the battery was produced at location $B,$\n\n- let $C$  be the event that the battery was produced at location $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since  location $A$ produces $30%$ of the batteries,  location $B$ produces $38%$ of the batteries, and location $C$ produces $32%$ of the batteries, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.3,\\,P(B)=0.38,\\,P(C)=0.32.\n$$\n\n- Since $3%$ of the batteries produced at location $A$, $5%$ of the batteries produced at location $B$, and $5%$ of the batteries produced at  location $C,$ are defective, we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.03,\\,P(D|B)=0.05,\\,P(D|C)=0.05.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.03)(0.3)+(0.05)(0.38)+(0.05)(0.32) \\\\  & =0.009+0.019+0.016 \\\\  & =0.044.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $D$ be the event that the battery is defective, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the battery was produced at location $A,$\n\n- let $B$  be the event that the battery was produced at location $B,$\n\n- let $C$  be the event that the battery was produced at location $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since  location $A$ produces $30\\%$ of the batteries,  location $B$ produces $38\\%$ of the batteries, and location $C$ produces $32\\%$ of the batteries, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.3,\\,P(B)=0.38,\\,P(C)=0.32.\n$$\n\n- Since $3\\%$ of the batteries produced at location $A$, $5\\%$ of the batteries produced at location $B$, and $5\\%$ of the batteries produced at  location $C,$ are defective, we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.03,\\,P(D|B)=0.05,\\,P(D|C)=0.05.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.03)(0.3)+(0.05)(0.38)+(0.05)(0.32) \\\\  & =0.009+0.019+0.016 \\\\  & =0.044.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186436226 :question/problem "Among all the people that visited Bill's ice cream shop last weekend, $25%$ were females under $18,$ $20%$ were females between $18$ and $30,$ and $15%$ were females over $30.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected person who visited Bill's shop last weekend is female?" "Among all the people that visited Bill's ice cream shop last weekend, $25\\%$ were females under $18,$ $20\\%$ were females between $18$ and $30,$ and $15\\%$ were females over $30.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected person who visited Bill's shop last weekend is female?"] [:db/cas 17592186436227 :question/problem "A company manufactures three types of cars. Of the car population, $40%$ are model ${C}_{1},$  $35%$ are model ${C}_{2},$ and the remaining cars are model ${C}_{3}.$ If $6%$ of the model ${C}_{1},$ $4%$ of the model  ${C}_{2},$ and $2%$ of the model ${C}_{3}$ cars break down during the first year, what is the probability that a randomly selected car will break down during the first year?" "A company manufactures three types of cars. Of the car population, $40\\%$ are model ${C}_{1},$  $35\\%$ are model ${C}_{2},$ and the remaining cars are model ${C}_{3}.$ If $6\\%$ of the model ${C}_{1},$ $4\\%$ of the model  ${C}_{2},$ and $2\\%$ of the model ${C}_{3}$ cars break down during the first year, what is the probability that a randomly selected car will break down during the first year?"] [:db/cas 17592186436227 :question/worked-solution "Let $B$ be the event that a randomly selected car breaks down during the first year, and \n\n- let ${C}_{1}$ be the event that the car is of model ${C}_{1},$\n\n- let ${C}_{2}$ be the event that the car is of model ${C}_{2},$\n\n- let ${C}_{3}$ be the event that the car is of model ${C}_{3}.$\n\nThen the disjoint union ${C}_{1}⊔{C}_{2}⊔{C}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected car will break down during the first year, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B) & =P(B\\cap {C}_{1})+P(B\\cap {C}_{2})+P(B\\cap {C}_{3}) \\\\  & =P(B|{C}_{1})P({C}_{1})+P(B|{C}_{2})P({C}_{2})+P(B|{C}_{3})P({C}_{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40%$ of the cars produced by the company are model ${C}_{1},$ and $50%$ are model ${C}_{2},$  the model ${C}_{3}$ cars make up the remaining\n\n$$\n100%-(40%+35%)=25%.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nP({C}_{1})=0.4,\\,P({C}_{2})=0.35,\\,P({C}_{3})=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $6%$ of the cars of model ${C}_{1},$ $4%$ of those of model  ${C}_{2},$ and $2%$ of the cars of model ${C}_{3}$ break down during the first year, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|{C}_{1})=0.06,\\,P(B|{C}_{2})=0.04,\\,P(B|{C}_{3})=0.02.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected car breaks down during the first year is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B) & =P(B|{C}_{1})P({C}_{1})+P(B|{C}_{2})P({C}_{2})+P(B|{C}_{3})P({C}_{3}) \\\\  & =(0.06)(0.4)+(0.04)(0.35)+(0.02)(0.25) \\\\  & =0.024+0.014+0.005 \\\\  & =0.043.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $B$ be the event that a randomly selected car breaks down during the first year, and \n\n- let ${C}_{1}$ be the event that the car is of model ${C}_{1},$\n\n- let ${C}_{2}$ be the event that the car is of model ${C}_{2},$\n\n- let ${C}_{3}$ be the event that the car is of model ${C}_{3}.$\n\nThen the disjoint union ${C}_{1}⊔{C}_{2}⊔{C}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected car will break down during the first year, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B) & =P(B\\cap {C}_{1})+P(B\\cap {C}_{2})+P(B\\cap {C}_{3}) \\\\  & =P(B|{C}_{1})P({C}_{1})+P(B|{C}_{2})P({C}_{2})+P(B|{C}_{3})P({C}_{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40\\%$ of the cars produced by the company are model ${C}_{1},$ and $50\\%$ are model ${C}_{2},$  the model ${C}_{3}$ cars make up the remaining\n\n$$\n100\\%-(40\\%+35\\%)=25\\%.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nP({C}_{1})=0.4,\\,P({C}_{2})=0.35,\\,P({C}_{3})=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $6\\%$ of the cars of model ${C}_{1},$ $4\\%$ of those of model  ${C}_{2},$ and $2\\%$ of the cars of model ${C}_{3}$ break down during the first year, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|{C}_{1})=0.06,\\,P(B|{C}_{2})=0.04,\\,P(B|{C}_{3})=0.02.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected car breaks down during the first year is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B) & =P(B|{C}_{1})P({C}_{1})+P(B|{C}_{2})P({C}_{2})+P(B|{C}_{3})P({C}_{3}) \\\\  & =(0.06)(0.4)+(0.04)(0.35)+(0.02)(0.25) \\\\  & =0.024+0.014+0.005 \\\\  & =0.043.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186436228 :question/problem "In a gas station, $40%$ of the clients buy Regular gasoline, $45%$ buy Premium gasoline, and $15%$ buy Supreme gasoline. If $65%$ of the clients that purchase Regular gasoline paid with a credit card, $50%$ of the clients that buy Premium gasoline paid with a credit card, and $60%$ of the clients that buy Supreme gasoline paid with a credit card,  what is the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card?" "In a gas station, $40\\%$ of the clients buy Regular gasoline, $45\\%$ buy Premium gasoline, and $15\\%$ buy Supreme gasoline. If $65\\%$ of the clients that purchase Regular gasoline paid with a credit card, $50\\%$ of the clients that buy Premium gasoline paid with a credit card, and $60\\%$ of the clients that buy Supreme gasoline paid with a credit card,  what is the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card?"] [:db/cas 17592186436228 :question/worked-solution "Let  $C$ be the event that the client paid with a credit card, and\n\n- let $R$ be the event that the client bought Regular gasoline,\n\n- let $Q$  be the event that the client bought Premium gasoline,\n\n- let $S$ be the event that the client bought Supreme gasoline.\n\nThen the disjoint union $R⊔Q⊔S$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C\\cap R)+P(C\\cap Q)+P(C\\cap S) \\\\  & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|Q)P(Q)+P(C|S)P(S)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40%$ of the clients buy Regular gasoline, $45%$ of the clients buy Premium gasoline, and $15%$ of the clients buy Supreme gasoline, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.4,\\,P(Q)=0.45,\\,P(S)=0.15.\n$$\n\n- Since $65%$ of the clients that buy Regular gasoline, $50%$ of the clients that buy Premium gasoline, and $60%$ of the clients that buy Supreme gasoline,  paid with a credit card, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|R)=0.65,\\,P(C|Q)=0.5,\\,P(C|S)=0.6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|Q)P(Q)+P(C|S)P(S) \\\\  & =(0.65)(0.4)+(0.5)(0.45)+(0.6)(0.15) \\\\  & =0.26+0.225+0.009 \\\\  & =0.575.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let  $C$ be the event that the client paid with a credit card, and\n\n- let $R$ be the event that the client bought Regular gasoline,\n\n- let $Q$  be the event that the client bought Premium gasoline,\n\n- let $S$ be the event that the client bought Supreme gasoline.\n\nThen the disjoint union $R⊔Q⊔S$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C\\cap R)+P(C\\cap Q)+P(C\\cap S) \\\\  & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|Q)P(Q)+P(C|S)P(S)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate ea