 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p),$ where\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=20%=0.2,\\,n=45.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(45,0.2)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =45\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =9 \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =45\\cdot 0.2\\cdot (1-0.2) \\\\  & =7.2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p),$ where\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=20\\%=0.2,\\,n=45.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(45,0.2)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =45\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =9 \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =45\\cdot 0.2\\cdot (1-0.2) \\\\  & =7.2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186441122 :question/problem "Suppose we have a population of size $N=180,$ where $45%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,180.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1).$" "Suppose we have a population of size $N=180,$ where $45\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,180.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186441123 :question/problem "A children's soccer academy has $60$ children enrolled. From this population, $35%$ are left-footed. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population is left-footed} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,60.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=0).$" "A children's soccer academy has $60$ children enrolled. From this population, $35\\%$ are left-footed. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population is left-footed} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,60.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186441672 :question/problem "$70%$ of $80$ is equal to{{field-1}}." "$70\\%$ of $80$ is equal to{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186441672 :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is $80,$ and the percentage is $70%.$ \n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n70%=70÷100=0.7\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =70%\\times 80 \\\\  & =0.7\\times 80 \\\\  & =56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $70%$ of $80$ is $56.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n70%=\\frac{70}{100}=\\frac{70÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{7}{10}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =70%\\times 80 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}\\times 80 \\\\  & =7\\times \\frac{80}{10} \\\\  & =7\\times 8 \\\\  & =56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $70%$ of $80$ is $56.$" "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is $80,$ and the percentage is $70\\%.$ \n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n70\\%=70÷100=0.7\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =70\\%\\times 80 \\\\  & =0.7\\times 80 \\\\  & =56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $70\\%$ of $80$ is $56.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n70\\%=\\frac{70}{100}=\\frac{70÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{7}{10}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =70\\%\\times 80 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}\\times 80 \\\\  & =7\\times \\frac{80}{10} \\\\  & =7\\times 8 \\\\  & =56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $70\\%$ of $80$ is $56.$"] [:db/cas 17592186441673 :question/problem "$225%$ of $32$ is equal