:\n\n$$\n0.6\\%=\\frac{0.6}{100}=\\frac{0.6\\times 10}{100\\times 10}=\\frac{6}{1,000}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =0.6\\%\\times 25 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{1,000}\\times 25 \\\\  & =\\frac{150}{1,000} \\\\  & =0.15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.6\\%$ of $25$ is $0.15.$"] [:db/cas 17592186441680 :question/problem "What is $0.25%$ of $400?$" "What is $0.25\\%$ of $400?$"] [:db/cas 17592186441680 :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is $400,$ and the percentage is $0.25%.$ \n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n0.25%=0.25÷100=0.0025\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =0.25%\\times 400 \\\\  & =0.0025\\times 400 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.25%$ of $400$ is $1.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n0.25%=\\frac{0.25}{100}=\\frac{0.25\\times 100}{100\\times 100}=\\frac{25}{10,000}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =0.25%\\times 400 \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{10,000}\\times 400 \\\\  & =\\frac{10,000}{10,000} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.25%$ of $400$ is $1.$" "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is $400,$ and the percentage is $0.25\\%.$ \n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n0.25\\%=0.25÷100=0.0025\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =0.25\\%\\times 400 \\\\  & =0.0025\\times 400 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.25\\%$ of