}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | ${x}_{3}$ |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the two linearly independent solutions are already mutually orthogonal, we only need to normalize them. Hence, we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-6}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-7}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"] [:db/cas 17592186445316 :question/problem "Carmen, an experienced seamstress, bought $15\\,\\text{m}$ of drill fabric. The total she paid, expressed as a **decimal number**, was $$${{field-1}}." "Carmen, an experienced seamstress, bought $15\\,\\text{m}$ of drill fabric. The total she paid, expressed as a **decimal number**, was \\${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186445316 :question/worked-solution "To find out the amount of money that Carmen must pay, we need to multiply $15$ by $3.48.$\n\nWe proceed by multiplying the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{4}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,.4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere are $2$ decimal places in $3.48.$ \nTherefore, the product will also have $2$ decimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $5220$ and add a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places.\n\n$$\n52\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,\\underbrace{20}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Carmen must pay $$3.48\\times 15=$52.20.$" "To find out the amount of money that Carmen must pay, we need to multiply $15$ by $3.48.$\n\nWe proceed by multiplying the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{4}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,.4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere are $2$ decimal places in $3.48.$ \nTherefore, the product will also have $2$ decimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $5220$ and add a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places.\n\n$$\n52\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,\\underbrace{20}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Carmen must pay $\\$3.48\\times 15=\\$52.20.$"] [:db/cas 17592186445318 :question/problem "A meter of drill fabric costs $$3.48.$ If Anna bought one meter of cotton fabric and one meter of drill fabric, in total, she paid $$${{field-1}}." "A meter of drill fabric costs $\\$3.48.$ If Anna bought one meter of cotton fabric and one meter of drill fabric, in total, she paid \\${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186445318 :question/worked-solution "In part 2, we found that one meter of cotton fabric costs $$3.95.$\n\nTo find the total cost, we need to calculate the value of $3.95+3.48.$\n\nFirst, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n3.95+3.48=7.43.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total cost is $$7.43.$" "In part 2, we found that one meter of cotton fabric costs $\\$3.95.$\n\nTo find the total cost, we need to calculate the value of $3.95+3.48.$\n\nFirst, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n3.95+3.48=7.43.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total cost is $\\$7.43.$"] [:db/cas 17592186445320 :question/problem "Monica bought $10\\,\\text{m}$ of cotton fabric and paid $$39.50.$ What is the price per meter of cotton fabric?\n\nExpressed as a **decimal number**, a meter of cotton fabric costs $ \n{{field-1}}." "Monica bought $10\\,\\text{m}$ of cotton fabric and paid $\\$39.50.$ What is the price per meter of cotton fabric?\n\nExpressed as a **decimal number**, a meter of cotton fabric costs \\$ \n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186445320 :question/worked-solution "The meter of cotton fabric costs $$(39.50÷10).$\n\nTo perform this division, all we need to do is move the decimal point one place to the left:\n\n$$\n39.50\\to 3.950=3.95\n$$\n\nTherefore, $39.50÷10=3.95.$" "The meter of cotton fabric costs $$(39.50÷10).$\n\nTo perform this division, all we need to do is move the decimal point one place to the left:\n\n$$\n39.50\\to 3.950=3.95\n\n\nTherefore, $39.50÷10=3.95.$"] [:db/cas 17592186446309 :question/problem "Ffion has an authorized overdraft facility with her bank. Her bank balance on Monday was $-$43.74.$ To reduce her debt, Ffion decides to make a deposit of $$29.50$ into her bank account on the following Wednesday. What was Ffion's balance at the end of the day on Wednesday?" "Ffion has an authorized overdraft facility with her bank. Her bank balance on Monday was $-\\$43.74.$ To reduce her debt, Ffion decides to make a deposit of $\\$29.50$ into her bank account on the following Wednesday. What was Ffion's balance at the end of the day on Wednesday?"] [:db/cas 17592186446309 :question/worked-solution "To determine the balance at the end of the day on Wedensday, we must add the amount of money Ffion deposited to the starting balance. This gives\n\n$$\n-$43.74+$29.50=-$14.24\n$$\n\nThus, Ffion's balance at the end of the day on Wednesday was $-$14.24.$" "To determine the balance at the end of the day on Wedensday, we must add the amount of money Ffion deposited to the starting balance. This gives\n\n$$\n-$43.74+$29.50=-$14.24\n$$\n\nThus, Ffion's balance at the end of the day on Wednesday was $-\\$14.24.$"] [:db/cas 17592186446310 :question/problem "Angela has a bank account with ASB Bank, and she has an overdraft facility authorized by this bank. At the start of a certain week, her balance was $$75.75.$ At the end of that week, she decided to withdraw $$80$ to buy a sound system. What was Angela's balance at the end of that week?" "Angela has a bank account with ASB Bank, and she has an overdraft facility authorized by this bank. At the start of a certain week, her balance was $\\$75.75.$ At the end of that week, she decided to withdraw $\\$80$ to buy a sound system. What was Angela's balance at the end of that week?"] [:db/cas 17592186446310 :question/worked-solution "To find Angela's bank balance at the end of that week, we subtract the amount of money she withdrew from the starting balance. This gives\n\n$$\n$75.75-$80=-$4.25.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Angela's bank balance at the end of the week was $-$4.25.$" "To find Angela's bank balance at the end of that week, we subtract the amount of money she withdrew from the starting balance. This gives\n\n$$\n$75.75-$80=-$4.25.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Angela's bank balance at the end of the week was $-\\$4.25.$"] [:db/cas 17592186447205 :question/problem "A processor manufacturer determines that $2%$ of the processors it produces are defective. A quality control officer inspects a random sample of $500$ processors. What is the standard deviation of the number of defective processors found in the sample? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." "A processor manufacturer determines that $2\\%$ of the processors it produces are defective. A quality control officer inspects a random sample of $500$ processors. What is the standard deviation of the number of defective processors found in the sample? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186447205 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of defective processors.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=500$ processors\n\n- the probability of a processor being defective is $p=2%=0.02$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(500,0.02).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{np(1-p)}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the standard deviation of the number of defective processors is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{np(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(500)(0.02)(1-0.02)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9.8} \\\\  & =3.13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of defective processors.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=500$ processors\n\n- the probability of a processor being defective is $p=2\\%=0.02$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(500,0.02).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{np(1-p)}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number o