$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in dollars per month, and $t>0$ is the time in months since the start of a particular year.\n\nIf the price was $$25$ per barrel at the start of the year, which expression gives the price of crude oil $t$ months since the start of the year?" "The price of crude oil, measured in dollars per barrel, increases at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\frac{2}{1+3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in dollars per month, and $t>0$ is the time in months since the start of a particular year.\n\nIf the price was $\\$25$ per barrel at the start of the year, which expression gives the price of crude oil $t$ months since the start of the year?"] [:db/cas 17592186478411 :question/worked-solution "We're given the **rate of change** of the account's balance $r(t).$ To find the **net change** in the balance between the times $t=2$ and $t=4,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}20{e}^{0.05t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={20\\,\\frac{{e}^{0.05t}}{0.05}|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =400{e}^{0.05t}{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =400({e}^{0.2}-{e}^{0.1}) \\\\  & ≈46\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. \n\nTherefore, between years $t=2$ and $t=4,$ the account's balance increased by $$46\\,000.$" "We're given the **rate of change** of the account's balance $r(t).$ To find the **net change** in the balance between the times $t=2$ and $t=4,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}20{e}^{0.05t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={20\\,\\frac{{e}^{0.05t}}{0.05}|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =400{e}^{0.05t}{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =400({e}^{0.2}-{e}^{0.1}) \\\\  & ≈46\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. \n\nTherefore, between years $t=2$ and $t=4,$ the account's balance increased by $\\$46\\,000.$"] [:db/cas 17592186480651 :question/problem "Fred purchased his land for $200$ thousand dollars. The value of the land increases by $10%$ each year. How much will Fred's plot be worth after $4$ years? Round your answer to the nearest thousand dollars.\n\nAfter $4$ years, the land will be worth approximately \n{{field-1}}\n thousand dollars." "Fred purchased his land for $200$ thousand dollars. The value of the land increases by $10\\%$ each year. How much will Fred's plot be worth after $4$ years? Round your answer to the nearest thousand dollars.\n\nAfter $4$ years, the land will be worth approximately \n{{field-1}}\n thousand dollars."] [:db/cas 17592186480651 :question/worked-solution "The land initially costs $200$ thousand, which increases at a rate of $10%$ per year.\n\n$10%$ as a decimal is $0.1.$ Therefore, an increase of $10%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.1=1.1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n* After $1$ year, the value will be $200\\cdot (1.1)$ thousand dollars.\n\n* After $2$ years, the value will be $200\\cdot (1.1)^{2}$ thousand dollars.\n\n* After $3$ years, the value will be $200\\cdot (1.1)^{3}$ thousand dollars.\n\n* After $4$ years, the value will be $200\\cdot (1.1)^{4}$ thousand dollars.\n\nEvaluating the expression for the value after $4$ years, we get the following:\n\n$$\n200\\cdot (1.1)^{4}=292.82≈293,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, after $4$ years, the land will be worth approximately $293$ thousand dollars." "The land initially costs $200$ thousand, which increases at a rate of $10\\%$ per year.\n\n$10\\%$ as a decimal is $0.1.$ Therefore, an increase of $10\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.1=1.1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n* After $1$ year, the value will be $200\\cdot (1.1)$ thousand dollars.\n\n* After $2$ years, the value will be $200\\cdot (1.1)^{2}$ thousand dollars.\n\n* After $3$ years, the value will be $200\\cdot (1.1)^{3}$ thousand dollars.\n\n* After $4$ years, the value will be $200\\cdot (1.1)^{4}$ thousand dollars.\n\nEvaluating the expression for the value after $4$ years, we get the following:\n\n$$\n200\\cdot (1.1)^{4}