nd{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let $A$ be the event that a light bulb is of type A, $D$ be the event that a light bulb is defective, and ${F}_{1},{F}_{2},{F}_{3}$ be the event that a light bulb is produced in factory $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({F}_{1})=0.25 &  & P(D|{F}_{1})=0.02 \\\\  & P({F}_{2})=0.5 &  & P(D|{F}_{2})=0.03 \\\\  & P({F}_{3})=0.25 &  & P(D|{F}_{3})=0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${F}_{1}⊔{F}_{2}⊔{F}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({F}_{1})+P({F}_{2})+P({F}_{3})=0.25+0.5+0.25=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $40\\%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since $1\\%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.01.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen defective light bulb is of type A, which is represented as $P(A|D).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|D) & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D|{F}_{1})P({F}_{1})+P(D|{F}_{2})P({F}_{2})+P(D|{F}_{3})P({F}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.01)(0.4)}{(0.02)(0.25)+(0.03)(0.5)+(0.01)(0.25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.004}{0.0225} \\\\  & ≈0.178,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186487319 :question/problem "An English teacher believes that students who prefer print handwriting tend to perform better in essay writing. To prove her belief, she puts some students into three groups according to their average writing grades and asks them if they usually write in print.\n\n- $20%$ of students have low grades.\n\n- $65%$ of students have average grades, and $55%$ of those students usually write in print.\n\n- $15%$ of students have high grades, and $52%$ of those students usually write in print.\n\nIf $42%$ of students are female, $