.$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W)} \\\\ P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W|L)P(L)+P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|H)P(H)} \\\\ 0.56 & =\\frac{(0.8)(0.42)}{P(W|L)(0.2)+(0.55)(0.65)+(0.52)(0.15)} \\\\ 0.56 & =\\frac{0.336}{0.2P(W|L)+0.4355}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(W|L),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.56(0.2P(W|L)+0.4355) & =0.336 \\\\ 0.2P(W|L)+0.4355 & =\\frac{0.336}{0.56} \\\\ 0.2P(W|L)+0.4355 & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0.2P(W|L) & =\\frac{3}{5}-0.4355 \\\\ P(W|L) & =\\frac{1}{0.2}(\\frac{3}{5}-0.4355) \\\\ P(W|L) & ≈0.823\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $W$ be the event that a student writes in print, and $L,A,H$ be the event that a student has low, average, or high writing grades, respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(L)=0.2 \\\\  & P(A)=0.65 &  & P(W|A)=0.55 \\\\  & P(H)=0.15 &  & P(W|H)=0.52\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union $L⊔A⊔H$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP(L)+P(A)+P(H)=0.2+0.65+0.15=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $42\\%$ of students in the entire school are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.42.\n$$\n\n- Since $80\\%$ of females write in print, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|F)=0.8.\n$$\n\n- Since $56\\%$ of students that write in print are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F|W)=0.56.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen student with low grades writes in print, which is represented as $P(W|L).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W)} \\\\ P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W|L)P(L)+P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|H)P(H)} \\\\ 0.56 & =\\frac{(0.8)(0.42)}{P(W|L)(0.2)+(0.55)(0.65)+(0.52)(0.15)} \\\\ 0.56 & =\\frac{0.336}{0.2P(W|L)+0.4355}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nS