en student who writes in cursive is a female? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "An English teacher believes that students who prefer cursive handwriting tend to perform better in essay writing. To prove his belief, he puts some students into groups of three according to their average writing grades and asks them if they usually write in cursive.\n\n- $20\\%$ of students have low grades, and $12\\%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\n- $65\\%$ of students have average grades, and $15\\%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\n- $15\\%$ of students have high grades, and $17\\%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\nIf $56\\%$ of students are female, and $16\\%$ of female students write in cursive, what is the probability that a randomly chosen student who writes in cursive is a female? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186487364 :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $W$ be the event that the student writes in cursive, and $L,A,H$ be the events that the student has low, average, or high grades, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(L)=0.2 &  & P(W|L)=0.12 \\\\  & P(A)=0.65 &  & P(W|A)=0.15 \\\\  & P(H)=0.15 &  & P(W|H)=0.17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union $L⊔A⊔H$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP(L)+P(A)+P(H)=0.2+0.65+0.15=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $56%$ of students are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.56.\n$$\n\n- Since $16%$ of female students write in cursive, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|F)=0.16.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen student who writes in cursive is a female, which is represented as $P(F|W).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W|L)P(L)+P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|H)P(H)} \\\\