ath, we have\n\n$$\nP(M|B)=0.27.\n$$\n\n- Since $8%$ of students who are good at math started to play an instrument before $6$ years old, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|M)=0.08.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen teenager who plays the violin is good at math, which is represented as $P(M|{T}_{2}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|M) & =\\frac{P(M|B)P(B)}{P(M)} \\\\ P(B|M) & =\\frac{P(M|B)P(B)}{P(M|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(M|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(M|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\ 0.08 & =\\frac{(0.27)(0.12)}{(0.2)(0.6)+P(M|{T}_{2})(0.15)+(0.35)(0.65)} \\\\ 0.08 & =\\frac{0.0324}{0.3475+0.15P(M|{T}_{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(M|{T}_{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.08(0.3475+0.15P(M|{T}_{2})) & =0.0324 \\\\ 0.3475+0.15P(M|{T}_{2}) & =\\frac{0.0324}{0.08} \\\\ 0.3475+0.15P(M|{T}_{2}) & =\\frac{81}{200} \\\\ 0.15P(M|{T}_{2}) & =\\frac{81}{200}-0.3475 \\\\ P(M|{T}_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{0.15}(\\frac{81}{200}-0.3475) \\\\ P(M|{T}_{2}) & ≈0.383\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "Let $B$ be the event that a teenager started to play music before $6$ years old, $M$ be the event that a teenager is good at math, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a teenager plays piano, violin, or another instrument, respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.2 &  & P(M|{T}_{1})=0.6 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.15 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.65 &  & P(M|{T}_{3})=0.35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.2+0.15+0.65=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $12\\%$ of teenagers started to play before $6$ years old, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.12.\n$$\n\n- Since $27\\%$ of teenagers who started to play before $6$ years old are good at math, we have\n\