agency offers three itineraries, itinerary $1,$ itinerary $2,$ and itinerary $3.$ For each itinerary, customers can decide to travel using their own car or the agency's bus.\n\n- Itinerary $1$ is chosen by $60\\%$ of the customers, and $10\\%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n\n- Itinerary $2$ is chosen by $20\\%$ of the customers, and $25\\%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n\n- Itinerary $3$ is chosen by $20\\%$ of the customers.\n\nIf $30\\%$ of all customers travel with children, $50\\%$ of customers with children choose the bus, and $83\\%$ of customers that choose the bus travel with children, what is the probability that a randomly chosen customer that chooses itinerary $3$ takes the bus? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186487403 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be the event that a customer has children, $B$ be the event that a customer takes the bus, and ${I}_{1},{I}_{2},{I}_{3}$ be the event that a customer chooses itinerary $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({I}_{1})=0.6 &  & P(B|{I}_{1})=0.1 \\\\  & P({I}_{2})=0.2 &  & P(B|{I}_{2})=0.25 \\\\  & P({I}_{3})=0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${I}_{1}⊔{I}_{2}⊔{I}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({I}_{1})+P({I}_{2})+P({I}_{3})=0.6+0.2+0.2=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $30%$ of customers have children, we have\n\n$$\nP(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $50%$ of customers with children choose the bus, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|C)=0.5.\n$$\n\n- Since $83%$ of customers that choose the bus have children, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|B)=0.83.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen customer that chooses itinerary $3$ travels by bus, which is represented as $P(B|{I}_{3}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C|B) & =\\frac{P(B|C)P(C)}{P(B)} \\\\ P(C|B) & =\\frac{P(B|C)P(C)}{P(B|{I}_{1})P({I}_{1})+P(B|{I}_{2})P({I}_{2})+P(B|{I}_{3})P({I}_{3})} \\\\ 0.83 & =\\frac{(0.5)(0.3)}{(0.1)(0.6)+(0.25)(0.2)+P(B|{I}_{3})(0.2)} \\\\ 0.83 & =\\frac{0.15}{0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(B|{I}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.83(0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3})) & =0.15 \\\\ 0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.15}{0.83} \\\\ 0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{15}{83} \\\\ 0.2P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{15}{83}-0.11 \\\\ P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.2}(\\frac{15}{83}-0.11) \\\\ P(B|{I}_{3}) & ≈0.354\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "Let $C$ be the event that a customer has children, $B$ be the event that a customer takes the bus, and ${I}_{1},{I}_{2},{I}_{3}$ be the event that a customer chooses itinerary $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({I}_{1})=0.6 &  & P(B|{I}_{1})=0.1 \\\\  & P({I}_{2})=0.2 &  & P(B|{I}_{2})=0.25 \\\\  & P({I}_{3})=0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${I}_{1}⊔{I}_{2}⊔{I}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({I}_{1})+P({I}_{2})+P({I}_{3})=0.6+0.2+0.2=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $30\\%$ of customers have children, we have\n\n$$\nP(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $50\\%$ of customers with children choose the bus, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|C)=0.5.\n$$\n\n- Since $83\\%$ of customers that choose the bus have children, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|B)=0.83.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen customer that chooses itinerary $3$ travels by bus, which is represented as $P(B|{I}_{3}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C|B) & =\\frac{P(B|C)P(C)}{P(B)} \\\\ P(C|B) & =\\frac{P(B|C)P(C)}{P(B|{I}_{1})P({I}_{1})+P(B|{I}_{2})P({I}_{2})+P(B|{I}_{3})P({I}_{3})} \\\\ 0.83 & =\\frac{(0.5)(0.3)}{(0.1)(0.6)+(0.25)(0.2)+P(B|{I}_{3})(0.2)} \\\\ 0.83 & =\\frac{0.15}{0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(B|{I}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.83(0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3})) & =0.15 \\\\ 0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.15}{0.83} \\\\ 0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{15}{83} \\\\ 0.2P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{15}{83}-0.11 \\\\ P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.2}(\\frac{15}{83}-0.11) \\\\ P(B|{I}_{3}) & ≈0.354\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186489175 :question/worked-solution "The answer choices require interpretation as a percent, and so we use the growth rate formula.\n\nThe given expression is an exponential growth expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot (1+r)^{t}$, where:\n\n- $a=800$ represents the initial area covered by the  blueberry bushes\n\n- $1+r=1.1,$ where $r$ is the growth rate\n\n- $t$ is the number of years since $2015$\n\nSolving for $r$:\n\n$$\n1+r=1.1\\,⟹\\,r=0.1\n$$\n\nNow, $0.1$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $10%.$ Therefore, the area covered by the bushes grows at a rate of $10%$ per year." "The answer choices require interpretation as a percent, and so we use the growth rate formula.\n\nThe given expression is an exponential growth expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot (1+r)^{t}$, where:\n\n- $a=800$ represents the initial area covered by the  blueberry bushes\n\n- $1+r=1.1,$ where $r$ is the growth rate\n\n- $t$ is the number of years since $2015$\n\nSolving for $r$:\n\n$$\n1+r=1.1\\,⟹\\,r=0.1\n$$\n\nNow, $0.1$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $10\\%.$ Therefore, the area covered by the bushes grows at a rate of $10\\%$ per year."] [:db/cas 17592186489176 :question/problem "A chemist increased the concentration of a lab solution to $125%$ of its original level. An additional reagent then increased that new concentration by $20%.$ If the original concentration was $80$ units, how many additional units were added solely by the reagent?\n\nThe reagent added \n{{field-1}}\nadditional units to the concentration." "A chemist increased the concentration of a lab solution to $125\\%$ of its original level. An additional reagent then increased that new concentration by $20\\%.$ If the original concentration was $80$ units, how many additional units were added solely by the reagent?\n\nThe reagent added \n{{field-1}}\nadditional units to the concentration."] [:db/cas 17592186489176 :question/worked-solution "First, the new concentration is $125%$ of the original $(80$ units$):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}125%\\times 80 & =\\frac{125}{100}\\times 80 \\\\  & =1.25\\times 80 \\\\  & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the new concentration is $100$ units. Then, the increase due to the reagent is $20%$ of this amount:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20%\\times 100 & =\\frac{20}{100}\\times 100 \\\\  & =0.2\\times 100 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the reagent added $20$ additional units to the concentration." "First, the new concentration is $125\\%$ of the original $(80$ units$):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}125\\%\\times 80 & =\\frac{125}{100}\\times 80 \\\\  & =1.25\\times 80 \\\\  & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the new concentration is $100$ units. Then, the increase due to the reagent is $20\\%$ of this amount:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20\\%\\times 100 & =\\frac{20}{100}\\times 100 \\\\  & =0.2\\times 100 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the reagent added $20$ additional units to the concentration."] [:db/cas 17592186489179 :question/problem "An online course launched with $100$ initial students. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each week after launch, the number of students increased by $160%$ of the number of students at the end of the previous week.\n\nAccording to this model, how many students are there estimated to be after $3$ weeks?" "An online course launched with $100$ initial students. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each week after launch, the number of students increased by $160\\%$ of the number of students at the end of the previous week.\n\nAccording to this model, how many students are there estimated to be after $3$ weeks?"] [:db/cas 17592186489179 :question/worked-solution "The number of students $S(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $160%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=100$ is the total number of students initially\n\n- $r=1.6$ is the growth rate ($160%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in weeks) after the launch\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nS=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $S(t)$ is\n\n$$\nS=100\\cdot (1+1.6)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nS(t)=100\\cdot (2.6)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of students after $3$ weeks, we evaluate the above for $t=3:$\n\n$$\nS(3)=100\\cdot (2.6)^{3}≈1758\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of students after $3$ weeks is $1758.$" "The number of students $S(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $160\\%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=100$ is the total number of students initially\n\n- $r=1.6$ is the growth rate ($160\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in weeks) after the launch\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nS=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $S(t)$ is\n\n$$\nS=100\\cdot (1+1.6)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nS(t)=100\\cdot (2.6)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of students after $3$ weeks, we evaluate the above for $t=3:$\n\n$$\nS(3)=100\\cdot (2.6)^{3}≈1758\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of students after $3$ weeks is $1758.$"] [:db/cas 17592186489206 :question/problem "A computer has $20$ files infected with a computer virus. The number of infected files $y(t)$ after $t$ days is given by the formula:\n\n$$\ny(t)=20\\cdot (\\text{___})^{t}\n$$\n\nIf the number of infected files increases $5%$ each day, then what is the missing value in the expression for $y?$" "A computer has $20$ files infected with a computer virus. The number of infected files $y(t)$ after $t$ days is given by the formula:\n\n$$\ny(t)=20\\cdot (\\text{___})^{t}\n$$\n\nIf the number of infected files increases $5\\%$ each day, then what is the missing value in the expression for $y?$"] [:db/cas 17592186489206 :question/worked-solution "The number of infected files $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $5%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=20$ is the initial number of infected files.\n\n- $r=0.05$ is the growth rate ($5%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in days.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =20\\cdot (1+0.05)^{t} \\\\  & =20\\cdot (1.05)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $1.05.$" "The number of infected files $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $5\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=20$ is the initial number of infected files.\n\n- $r=0.05$ is the growth rate ($5\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in days.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =20\\cdot (1+0.05)^{t} \\\\  & =20\\cdot (1.05)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $1.05.$"] [:db/cas 17592186489225 :question/problem "According to a recent report, $60%$ of tablet owners said they used their tablets regularly. Of those, $50%$ said they used them primarily for drawing. What percentage of all tablet owners used tablets primarily for drawing?" "According to a recent report, $60\\%$ of tablet owners said they used their tablets regularly. Of those, $50\\%$ said they used them primarily for drawing. What percentage of all tablet owners used tablets primarily for drawing?"] [:db/cas 17592186489225 :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the total number of tablet owners.\n\nWe're told that $60%$ of tablet owners used their tablets regularly. Thus, the number o