ch week.\n\n$2%$ as a decimal is $0.02.$ Therefore, an increase of $2%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.02=1.02.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ week, the price will be $2\\cdot (1.02)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ weeks, the price will be $2\\cdot (1.02)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ weeks, the price will be $2\\cdot (1.02)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n- After $4$ weeks, the price will be $2\\cdot (1.02)^{4}$ dollars." "The price of a gallon of gasoline is $\\$2$, and this amount will increase by $2\\%$ each week.\n\n$2\\%$ as a decimal is $0.02.$ Therefore, an increase of $2\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.02=1.02.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ week, the price will be $2\\cdot (1.02)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ weeks, the price will be $2\\cdot (1.02)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ weeks, the price will be $2\\cdot (1.02)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n- After $4$ weeks, the price will be $2\\cdot (1.02)^{4}$ dollars."] [:db/cas 17592186491120 :question/problem "Max initially had $$2$ in his piggy bank. Then, thanks to a generous relative, the amount of money in Max's piggy bank doubled at the end of every day. How much money will Max have in the piggy bank after $6$ days?" "Max initially had $\\$2$ in his piggy bank. Then, thanks to a generous relative, the amount of money in Max's piggy bank doubled at the end of every day. How much money will Max have in the piggy bank after $6$ days?"] [:db/cas 17592186491120 :question/worked-solution "Initially, Max has $$2$ in the piggy bank, and this amount doubles every day.\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ day, Max has $2\\cdot 2$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ days, Max will have $2\\cdot (2)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ days, Max will have $2\\cdot (2)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n- $…$\n\n- After $6$ days, Max will have $2\\cdot (2)^{6}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the expression for its value after $6$ days, we get:\n\n$$\n2\\cdot (2)^{6}=2\\cdot 64=128\n$$\n\nThus, Max will have $$128$ after $6$ days." "Initially, Max has $\\$2$ in the piggy bank, and this amount doubles every day.\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ day, Max has $2\\cdot 2$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ days, Max will have $2\\cdot (2)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ days, Max will have $2\\cdot (2)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n- $…$\n\n- After $6$ days, Max will have $2\\cdot (2)^{6}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the expression for its value after $6$ days, we get:\n\n$$\n2\\cdot (2)^{6}=2\\cdot 64=128\n$$\n\nThus, Max will have $\\$128$ after $6$ days."] [:db/cas 17592186491124 :question/problem "Mary is randomly picking a candy from a bag and putting it back in until she gets a lemon-flavored candy. Approximately, $6%$ of the candies in the bag are lemon-flavored. What is the probability that the $9$th candy is the first lemon-flavored candy picked? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "Mary is randomly picking a candy from a bag and putting it back in until she gets a lemon-flavored candy. Approximately, $6\\%$ of the candies in the bag are lemon-flavored. What is the probability that the $9$th candy is the first lemon-flavored candy picked? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186491124 :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of picked candies until a lemon-flavored candy is obtained. Since the chance to get a lemon-flavored candy is $6%,$ the number of picked candies until a lemon-flavored candy is obtained can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=6%=\\frac{6}{100}=0.06.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.06).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.06)^{x-1}(0.06) \\\\  & =(0.94)^{x-1}(0.06)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the $9$th attempt is the first lemon-flavored candy is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=9) & =f(9) \\\\  & =(0.94)^{9-1}(0.06) \\\\  & =(0.94)^{8}(0.06) \\\\  & ≈0.037\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of picked candies until a lemon-flavored candy is obtained. Since the chance to get a lemon-flavored candy is $6\\%,$ the number of picked candies until a lemon-flavored candy is obtained can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=6\\%=\\frac{6}{100}=0.06.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.06).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.06)^{x-1}(0.06) \\\\  & =(0.94)^{x-1}(0.06)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the $9$th attempt is the first lemon-flavored candy is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=9) & =f(9) \\\\  & =(0.94)^{9-1}(0.06) \\\\  & =(0.94)^{8}(0.06) \\\\  & ≈0.037\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186491138 :question/problem "A basketball player has a $70%$ chance of making a free throw on any given attempt. The player continues shooting free throws until they make a shot. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that the player will attempt fewer than $4$ free throws is{{field-1}}." "A basketball player has a $70\\%$ chance of making a free throw on any given attempt. The player continues shooting free throws until they make a shot. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that the player will attempt fewer than $4$ free throws is{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186491138 :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of free throws attempted until the player made a shot. Since the chance of making a free throw is $70%,$ the number of free throws attempted until the player makes a shot can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=70%=\\frac{70}{100}=0.7.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.7).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.7)^{x-1}(0.7) \\\\  & =(0.3)^{x-1}(0.7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the player will attempt fewer than $4$ free throws is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.3)^{0}(0.7)+(0.3)^{1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{2}(0.7) \\\\  & =0.7+0.21+0.063 \\\\  & =0.973.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of free throws attempted until the player made a shot. Since the chance of making a free throw is $70\\%,$ the number of free throws attempted until the player makes a shot can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=70\\%=\\frac{70}{100}=0.7.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.7).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.7)^{x-1}(0.7) \\\\  & =(0.3)^{x-1}(0.7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the player will attempt fewer than $4$ free throws is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.3)^{0}(0.7)+(0.3)^{1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{2}(0.7) \\\\  & =0.7+0.21+0.063 \\\\  & =0.973.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186491139 :question/problem "A pastry shop is famous for its chocolate cakes. On average, only $20%$ of the shop's customers prefer to buy a type of cake other than chocolate. What is the probability that the first customer who decides not to buy a chocolate cake appears before the $4$th customer?" "A pastry shop is famous for its chocolate cakes. On average, only $20\\%$ of the shop's customers prefer to buy a type of cake