ompute $P(X>3),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>3)=1-P(X\\le 3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.1)^{1-1}(0.9)+(0.1)^{2-1}(0.9)+(0.1)^{3-1}(0.9) \\\\  & =(0.9)+(0.1)(0.9)+(0.1)^{2}(0.9) \\\\  & =[1+0.1+(0.1)^{2}](0.9) \\\\  & =0.999.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the first passing test will begin being graded after grading $3$ tests is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & =1-0.999 \\\\  & =0.001.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of graded tests until a passing test starts to be graded. Since the chance of passing the test is $90\\%,$ the number of graded tests until starting to grade the first passing one can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=90\\%=\\frac{90}{100}=0.9.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.9).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.9)^{x-1}(0.9) \\\\  & =(0.1)^{x-1}(0.9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>3),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>3)=1-P(X\\le 3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.1)^{1-1}(0.9)+(0.1)^{2-1}(0.9)+(0.1)^{3-1}(0.9) \\\\  & =(0.9)+(0.1)(0.9)+(0.1)^{2}(0.9) \\\\  & =[1+0.1+(0.1)^{2}](0.9) \\\\  & =0.999.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the first passing test will begin being graded after grading $3$ tests is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & =1-0.999 \\\\  & =0.001.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186491155 :question/problem "In a coffee shop, $30%$ of customers buy a piece of cake. What is the probability that the $5$th customer of the day is the fi