r of the day is the first buying a piece of cake is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =(0.7)^{5-1}(0.3) \\\\  & =(0.7)^{4}(0.3) \\\\  & ≈0.072\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186491157 :question/problem "Jacob purchased his land for $180$ thousand dollars. The value of the land increases by $8%$ each year. How much will Jacob's plot be worth after $4$ years? Round your answer to the nearest thousand dollars.\n\nAfter $4$ years, the land will be worth approximately \n{{field-1}}\n thousand dollars." "Jacob purchased his land for $180$ thousand dollars. The value of the land increases by $8\\%$ each year. How much will Jacob's plot be worth after $4$ years? Round your answer to the nearest thousand dollars.\n\nAfter $4$ years, the land will be worth approximately \n{{field-1}}\n thousand dollars."] [:db/cas 17592186491157 :question/worked-solution "The land initially costs $$180$ thousand, which increases at a rate of $8%$ per year.\n\n$8%$ as a decimal is $0.08.$ Therefore, an increase of $8%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.08=1.08.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n* After $1$ year, the value will be $180\\cdot (1.08)$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $2$ years, the value will be $180\\cdot (1.08)^{2}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $3$ years, the value will be $180\\cdot (1.08)^{3}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $4$ years, the value will be $180\\cdot (1.08)^{4}$ thousand dollars\n\nEvaluating the expression for the value after $4$ years, we get the following:\n\n$$\n180\\cdot (1.08)^{4}=244.88≈245,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, after $4$ years, the land will be worth approximately $245$ thousand dollars." "The land initially costs $\\$180$ thousand, which increases at a rate of $8\\%$ per year.\n\n$8\\%$ as a decimal is $0.08.$ Therefore, an increase of $8\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.08=1.08.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n* After $1$ year, the value will be $180\\cdot (1.08)$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $2$ years, the value will be $180\\cdot (1.08)^{2}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $3$ years, the value will be $180\\cdot (1.08)^{3}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $4$ years, the value will be $180\\cdot (1.08)^{4}$ thousand dollars\n\nEvaluating the expression for the value after $4$ years, we get the following:\n\n$$\n180\\cdot (1.08)^{4}=244.88≈245,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, after $4$ years, the land will be worth approximately $245$ thousand dollars."] [:db/cas 17592186491176 :question/problem "A salesperson has a $72%$ chance of closing a sale on any given call. The salesperson continues making calls until they close a sale. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that they will make exactly $6$ calls is{{field-1}}." "A salesperson has a $72\\%$ chance of closing a sale on any given call. The salesperson continues making calls until they close a sale. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that they will make exactly $6$ calls is{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186491176 :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of calls made until the salesperson closes a sale. Since the chance of closing a sale is $72%,$ the number of calls until the salesperson closes a sale can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=72%=\\frac{72}{100}=0.72.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.72).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.72)^{x-1}(0.72) \\\\  & =(0.28)^{x-1}(0.72)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the salesperson will close their first sale on the $6$th call is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=6) & =f(6) \\\\  & =(0.28)^{6-1}(0.72) \\\\  & =(0.28)^{5}(0.72) \\\\  & ≈0.001,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of calls made until the salesperson closes a sale. Since the chance of closing a sale is $72\\%,$ the number of calls until the salesperson closes a sale can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=72\\%=\\frac{72}{100}=0.72.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.72).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.72)^{x-1}(0.72) \\\\  & =(0.28)^{x-1}(0.72)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the salesperson will close their first sale on the $6$th call is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=6) & =f(6) \\\\  & =(0.28)^{6-1}(0.72) \\\\  & =(0.28)^{5}(0.72) \\\\  & ≈0.001,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186491183 :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ of $n$ coins is drawn from a population. The population has coins of two different denominations, $$1$ and $$0.50,$ distributed in the ratio $1:4.$ Determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $$0.02.$" "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ of $n$ coins is drawn from a population. The population has coins of two different denominations, $\\$1$ and $\\$0.50,$ distributed in the ratio $1:4.$ Determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $\\$0.02.$"] [:db/cas 17592186491183 :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the coins in the population have denominations $$1$ and $$0.50,$ and these are distributed in the ratio $1:4.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $1+4=5$ coins only with the following denominations:\n\n$$\n$1,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+4\\cdot 0.5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =$0.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({1}^{2}+4\\cdot {0.5}^{2})-{0.6}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2)-0.36 \\\\  & =0.4-0.36 \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n\n$$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.04}=0.2,$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{0.2}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $$0.02.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.2}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.02 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{0.2}{0.02} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >10 \\\\ n & >{10}^{2} \\\\ n & >100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=101.$" "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the coins in the population have denominations $\\$1$ and $\\$0.50,$ and these are distributed in the ratio $1:4.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $1+4=5$ coins only with the following denominations:\n\n$$\n$1,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+4\\cdot 0.5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =$0.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({1}^{2}+4\\cdot {0.5}^{2})-{0.6}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2)-0.36 \\\\  & =0.4-0.36 \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.04}=0.2,$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{0.2}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $\\$0.02.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.2}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.02 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{0.2}{0.02} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >10 \\\\ n & >{10}^{2} \\\\ n & >100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=101.$"] [:db/cas 17592186491195 :question/problem "On the opening day of the children's baseball season, $300$ people attended the baseball game. The next game was attended by $120%$ of the number of people who attended the opening game. How many people attended the second game?" "On the opening day of the children's baseball season, $300$ people attended the baseball game. The next game was attended by $120\\%$ of the number of people who attended the opening game. How many people attended the second game?"] [:db/cas 17592186491195 :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of people who attended the opening game, which is $300$ people. The percentage is $120%,$ and the part is the number of people who attended the second game.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n120%=120÷100=1.2\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120%\\times 300 \\\\  & =1.2\\times 300 \\\\  & =360\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120%$ of $300$ is $360.$ So, $360$ people attended the second game.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n120%=\\frac{120}{100}=\\frac{120÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120%\\times 300 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\times 300 \\\\  & =6\\times \\frac{300}{5} \\\\  & =6\\times 60 \\\\  & =360\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120%$ of $300$ is $360.$ So, $360$ people attended the second game." "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of people who attended the opening game, which is $300$ people. The percentage is $120\\%,$ and the part is the number of people who attended the second game.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n120\\%=120÷100=1.2\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120\\%\\times 300 \\\\  & =1.2\\times 300 \\\\  & =360\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120\\%$ of $300$ is $360.$ So, $360$ people attended the second game.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n120\\%=\\frac{120}{100}=\\frac{120÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120\\%\\times 300 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\times 300 \\\\  & =6\\times \\frac{300}{5} \\\\  & =6\\times 60 \\\\  & =360\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120\\%$ of $300$ is $360.$ So, $360$ people attended the second game."] [:db/cas 17592186491434 :question/worked-solution "The slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=1$ is given by $f'(1).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = \\sin{u}, \\qquad u = \\pi \\left(8x^{2} +2x - 2\\right).\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\cos{u}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\pi (16x + 2).\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\cos u \\cdot \\pi (16x+2)\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (16x+2)\\cos u\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (16x+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(8x^{2} +2x - 2\\right) \\right).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we find\n\n\\begin{align}\nf'(1) & =\\pi (16\\cdot1+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(8\\cdot1^{2} +2\\cdot1 - 2\\right) \\right) \\\\[3pt]\n\n& =18\\pi \\cos(8\\pi) \\\\[3pt]\n&= 18\\pi \\cdot (1) \\\\[3pt]\n&= 18 \\pi\n\\end{align}\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is \n\\[\nm = f'(1) = \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{18 \\pi}.\n\\]" "The slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=1$ is given by $f'(1).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = \\sin{u}, \\qquad u = \\pi \\left(8x^{2} +2x - 2\\right).\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\cos{u}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\pi (16x + 2).\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\cos u \\cdot \\pi (16x+2)\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (16x+2)\\cos u\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (16x+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(8x^{2} +2x - 2\\right) \\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(1) & =\\pi (16\\cdot1+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(8\\cdot1^{2} +2\\cdot1 - 2\\right) \\right) \\\\[3pt]\n\n& =18\\pi \\cos(8\\pi) \\\\[3pt]\n&= 18\\pi \\cdot (1) \\\\[3pt]\n&= 18 \\pi\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is \n\\[\nm = f'(1) = \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{18 \\pi}.\n\\]"] [:db/cas 17592186492892 :question/problem "Three factories produce the same tool for the market. Factory $A$ produces $30%$ of the tools, of which $2%$ are defective. Factory $B$ produces $34%$ of the tools, of which $5%$ are defective, and factory $C$ produces $36%$ of the tools, of which $5%$ are defective. What is the probability that a randomly selected tool is defective?" "Three factories produce the same tool for the market. Factory $A$ produces $30\\%$ of the tools, of which $2\\%$ are defective. Factory $B$ produces $34\\%$ of the tools, of which $5\\%$ are defective, and factory $C$ produces $36\\%$ of the tools, of which $5\\%$ are defective. What is the probability that a randomly selected tool is defective?"] [:db/cas 17592186492892 :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected tool is defective, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the tool was produced by factory $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the tool was produced by factory $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the tool was produced by factory $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected tool is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since factory $A$ produces $30%$ of the tools, factory $B$ produces $34%$ of the tools, and factory $C$ produces $36%$ of the tools, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.3,\\,P(B)=0.34,\\,P(C)=0.36.\n$$\n\n- Since $2%$ of the tools produced by factory $A$, $5%$ of the tools produced by factory $B$, and $5%$ of the tools produced by factory $C,$ are defective, we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.02,\\,P(D|B)=0.05,\\,P(D|C)=0.05.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected tool is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.02)(0.3)+(0.05)(0.34)+(0.05)(0.36) \\\\  & =0.006+0.017+0.018 \\\\  & =0.041.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected tool is defective, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the tool was produced by factory $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the tool was produced by factory $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the tool was produced by factory $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected tool is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since factory $A$ produces $30\\%$ of the tools, factory $B$ produces $34\\%$ of the tools, and factory $C$ produces $36\\%$ of the tools, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.3,\\,P(B)=0.34,\\,P(C)=0.36.\n$$\n\n- Since $2\\%$ of the tools produced by factory $A$, $5\\%$ of the tools produced by factory $B$, and $5\\%$ of the tools produced by factory $C,$ are defective, we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.02,\\,P(D|B)=0.05,\\,P(D|C)=0.05.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected tool is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.02)(0.3)+(0.05)(0.34)+(0.05)(0.36) \\\\  & =0.006+0.017+0.018 \\\\  & =0.041.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186492894 :question/problem "Ann has three email accounts. In the first account, she receives $40%$ of her emails, of which $10%$ are spam. In the second account, she receives $35%$ of her emails, of which $8%$ are spam. And in the third account, she receives $25%$ of her emails, of 