begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(S|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(S|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3}) \\\\  & =(0.1)(0.4)+(0.08)(0.35)+(0.08)(0.25) \\\\  & =0.04+0.028+0.02 \\\\  & =0.088.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $S$ be the event that the selected email is spam, and\n\n- let ${A}_{1}$ be the event that the email was received in the first account,\n\n- let ${A}_{2}$ be the event that the email was received in the second account,\n\n- let ${A}_{3}$ be the event that the email was received in the third account.\n\nThen the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected email is spam, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S\\cap {A}_{1})+P(S\\cap {A}_{2})+P(S\\cap {A}_{3}) \\\\  & =P(S|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(S|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(S|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since Ann receives $40\\%$ of her emails in the first account,  $35\\%$ in the second account, and $25\\%$ in the third account, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})=0.4,\\,P({A}_{2})=0.35,\\,P({A}_{3})=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $10\\%$ of the emails received in the first account, $8\\%$ of the emails received in the first account, and $8\\%$ of the emails received in the third account are spam, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|{A}_{1})=0.1,\\,P(S|{A}_{2})=0.08\\,P(S|{A}_{3})=0.08.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected email from any of Ann's accounts is spam is   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(S|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(S|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3}) \\\\  & =(0.1)(0.4)+(0.08)(0.35)+(0.08)(0.25) \\\\  & =0.04+0.028+0.02 \\\\  & =0.088.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186492923 :question/problem "Catherine sold $6$ identical bottles of perfume, for which she received a total of $$90.33.$ Estimate the price of each bottle by rounding the amount of money Catherine received to the nearest dollar.\n\nThe approximate price for each bottle is $\n{{field-1}}." "Catherine sold $6$ identical bottles of perfume, for which she received a total of $\\$90.33.$ Estimate the price of each bottle by rounding the amount of money Catherine received to the nearest dollar.\n\nThe approximate price for each bottle is \\$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186492923 :question/worked-solution "To estimate the price of each of the $6$ perfumes, we need to find an approximation for $90.33÷6.$\n\nSo, we round the dividend to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n90.33\\to 90\n$$\n\nNow, we divide $90$ by $6$ as usual:\n\n$$\n90÷6=15\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximate price for each bottle is $$15.$" "To estimate the price of each of the $6$ perfumes, we need to find an approximation for $90.33÷6.$\n\nSo, we round the dividend to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n90.33\\to 90\n$$\n\nNow, we divide $90$ by $6$ as usual:\n\n$$\n90÷6=15\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximate price for each bottle is $\\$15.$"] [:db/cas 17592186493898 :question/problem "The weekly exercise time for a community is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ minutes and a standard deviation of $30$ minutes. Additionally, $60%$ of people exercise between $0.9\\mu $ minutes and $1.1\\mu $ minutes each week. Find the percentage of people who exercise less than $220$ minutes per week. **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | 