 ) & =0.60 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.30 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.5+0.30 \\\\ P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.80 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.1\\mu }{30}) & =0.80 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{0.1\\mu }{30}\\right) & =0.80 \\\\ \\frac{0.1\\mu }{30} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.80).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.80)=0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.1\\mu }{30} & =0.8416 \\\\ 0.1\\mu  & =25.248 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{25.248}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈252.48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<220)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<220) & =P(Z<\\frac{220-252.48}{30}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.08) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.08) \\\\  & =0.1401 \\\\  & ≈14.01%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $14.01%$ of the people exercise less than $220$ minutes per week." "Let $X$ represent the weekly exercise time of a randomly selected person. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{30}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<220).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu )=0.60.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.30 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.5+0.30 \\\\ P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.80 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.1\\mu }{30}) & =0.80 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{0.1\\mu }{30}\\right) & =0.80 \\\\ \\frac{0.1\\mu }{30} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.80).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.80)=0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.1\\mu }{30} & =0.8416 \\\\ 0.1\\mu  & =25.248 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{25.248}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈252.48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<220)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<220) & =P(Z<\\frac{220-252.48}{30}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.08) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.08) \\\\  & =0.1401 \\\\  & ≈14.01\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $14.01\\%$ of the people exercise less than $220$ minutes per week."] [:db/cas 17592186493899 :question/problem "A group of students took a test and got an average score of $81.$ Additionally, $5%$ of the students scored greater than $91.$ Assuming that the students' scores are normally distributed, find the percent of students with a score lower than $70,$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.900\\,$ | $\\,0.950\\,$ | $\\,0.975\\,$ | $\\,0.990\\,$ | $\\,0.995\\,$ | $\\,0.999\\,$ | $\\,0.9995\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0287\\,$ | $\\,0.0281\\,$ | $\\,0.0274\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0359\\,$ | $\\,0.0351\\,$ | $\\,0.0344\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0446\\,$ | $\\,0.0436\\,$ | $\\,0.0427\\,$ |" "A group of students took a test and got an average score of $81.$ Additionally, $5\\%$ of the students scored greater than $91.$ Assuming that the students' scores are normally distributed, find the percent of students with a score lower than $70,$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.900\\,$ | $\\,0.950\\,$ | $\\,0.975\\,$ | $\\,0.990\\,$ | $\\,0.995\\,$ | $\\,0.999\\,$ | $\\,0.9995\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0287\\,$ | $\\,0.0281\\,$ | $\\,0.0274\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0359\\,$ | $\\,0.0351\\,$ | $\\,0.0344\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0446\\,$ | $\\,0.0436\\,$ | $\\,0.0427\\,$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186493899 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the grade obtained by a student. Then we have $X∼N(81,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<70).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>91)=0.05.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 91) & =1-P(X>91) \\\\  & =1-0.05 \\\\  & =0.95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 91) & =0.95 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{91-81}{\\sigma }) & =0.95 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{10}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.95 \\\\ \\frac{10}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.95)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.95)≈1.6449.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10}{\\sigma } & =1.6449 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.16449 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.16449} \\\\  & ≈6.0794.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<70)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<70) & =P(X\\le 70) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{70-81}{6.0794}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.81) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.81) \\\\  & =0.0351 \\\\  & ≈3.5%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $3.5%$ of the students scored lower than $70.$" "Let $X$ represent the grade obtained by a student. Then we have $X∼N(81,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<70).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>91)=0.05.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 91) & =1-P(X>91) \\\\  & =1-0.05 \\\\  & =0.95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 91) & =0.95 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{91-81}{\\sigma }) & =0.95 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{10}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.95 \\\\ \\frac{10}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.95)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.95)≈1.6449.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10}{\\sigma } & =1.6449 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.16449 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.16449} \\\\  & ≈6.0794.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<70)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<70) & =P(X\\le 70) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{70-81}{6.0794}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.81) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.81) \\\\  & =0.0351 \\\\  & ≈3.5\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $3.5\\%$ of the students scored lower than $70.$"] [:db/cas 17592186493903 :question/problem "In a nature park, there are $4$ species of squirrels, each with the same population.\n\n- Among the squirrels of species $1,$ $12%$ are born in captivity.\n\n- Among the squirrels of species $2,$ $5%$ are born in captivity.\n\n- Among the squirrels of species $3,$ $6%$ are born in captivity.\n\n- Among the squirrels of species $4,$ $3%$ are born in captivity.\n\nIf $35%$ of the squirrels are adult females, and $8%$ of the adult female squirrels are born in captivity, what is the probability that a randomly chosen squirrel born in captivity is an adult female? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "In a nature park, there are $4$ species of squirrels, each with the same population.\n\n- Among the squirrels of species $1,$ $12\\%$ are born in captivity.\n\n- Among the squirrels of species $2,$ $5\\%$ are born in captivity.\n\n- Among the squirrels of species $3,$ $6\\%$ are born in captivity.\n\n- Among the squirrels of species $4,$ $3\\%$ are born in captivity.\n\nIf $35\\%$ of the squirrels are adult females, and $8\\%$ of the adult female squirrels are born in captivity, what is the probability that a randomly chosen squirrel born in captivity is an adult female? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186493903 :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a squirrel is an adult female, $C$ be the event that a squirrel is born in captivity, and ${S}_{1},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},{S}_{4}$ be the event that a squirrel is of species $1,$ $2,$ $3,$ or $4,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- Each species has the same number of squirrels, so we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({S}_{1})=P({S}_{2})=P({S}_{3})=P({S}_{4})=0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(C|{S}_{1})=0.12 \\\\  & P(C|{S}_{2})=0.05 \\\\  & P(C|{S}_{3})=0.06 \\\\  & P(C|{S}_{4})=0.03\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}⊔{S}_{3}⊔{S}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})+P({S}_{3})+P({S}_{4})=0.25+0.25+0.25+0.25=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $35%$ of the squirrels are adult females, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.35.\n$$\n\n- Since $8%$ of adult females are born in captivity, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|F)=0.08.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen squirrel born in captivity is an adult female, which is represented as $P(F|C).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|C) & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C|{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(C|{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})+P(C|{S}_{3})P({S}_{3})+P(C|{S}_{3})P({S}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.08)(0.35)}{(0.12)(0.25)+(0.05)(0.25)+(0.06)(0.25)+(0.03)(0.25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.028}{0.065} \\\\  & ≈0.431,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let $F$ be the event that a squirrel is an adult female, $C$ be the event that a squirrel is born in captivity, and ${S}_{1},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},{S}_{4}$ be the event that a squirrel is of species $1,$ $2,$ $3,$ or $4,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- Each species has the same number of squirrels, so we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({S}_{1})=P({S}_{2})=P({S}_{3})=P({S}_{4})=0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(C|{S}_{1})=0.12 \\\\  & P(C|{S}_{2})=0.05 \\\\  & P(C|{S}_{3})=0.06 \\\\  & P(C|{S}_{4})=0.03\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}⊔{S}_{3}⊔{S}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})+P({S}_{3})+P({S}_{4})=0.25+0.25+0.25+0.25=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $35\\%$ of the squirrels are adult females, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.35.\n$$\n\n- Since $8\\%$ of adult females are born in captivity, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|F)=0.08.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen squirrel born in captivity is an adult female, which is represented as $P(F|C).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|C) & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C|{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(C|{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})+P(C|{S}_{3})P({S}_{3})+P(C|{S}_{3})P({S}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.08)(0.35)}{(0.12)(0.25)+(0.05)(0.25)+(0.06)(0.25)+(0.03)(0.25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.028}{0.065} \\\\  & ≈0.431,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186493912 :question/problem "A manufacturing company has three sites, factory $1,$ factory $2,$ and factory $3.$ They all produce two types of light bulbs: A and B.\n\n- Factory $1$ produces $45%$ of the entire production, and $5%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\n- Factory $2$ produces $45%$ of the entire production.\n\n- Factory $3$ produces $10%$ of the entire production, and $1%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\nIf $57%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, $3%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, and $41%$ of defective light bulbs are of type A, what is the probability that a randomly chosen light bulb from factory $2$ is defective? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "A manufacturing company has three sites, factory $1,$ factory $2,$ and factory $3.$ They all produce two types of light bulbs: A and B.\n\n- Factory $1$ produces $45\\%$ of the entire production, and $5\\%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\n- Factory $2$ produces $45\\%$ of the entire production.\n\n- Factory $3$ produces $10\\%$ of the entire production, and $1\\%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\nIf $57\\%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, $3\\%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, and $41\\%$ of defective light bulbs are of type A, what is the probability that a randomly chosen light bulb from factory $2$ is defective? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186493912 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the event that a light bulb is of type A, $D$ be the event that a light bulb is defective, and ${F}_{1},{F}_{2},{F}_{3}$ be the event that a light bulb is produced in factory $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({F}_{1})=0.45 &  & P(D|{F}_{1})=0.05 \\\\  & P({F}_{2})=0.45 \\\\  & P({F}_{3})=0.1 &  & P(D|{F}_{3})=0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${F}_{1}⊔{F}_{2}⊔{F}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({F}_{1})+P({F}_{2})+P({F}_{3})=0.45+0.45+0.1=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $57%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.57.\n$$\n\n- Since $3%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.03.\n$$\n\n- Since $41%$ of defective light bulbs are of type A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A|D)=0.41\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen light bulb from factory $2$ is defective, which is represented as $P(D|{F}_{2}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|D) & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D)} \\\\ P(A|D) & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D|{F}_{1})P({F}_{1})+P(D|{F}_{2})P({F}_{2})+P(D|{F}_{3})P({F}_{3})} \\\\ 0.41 & =\\frac{(0.03)(0.57)}{(0.05)(0.45)+P(D|{F}_{2})(0.45)+(0.01)(0.1)} \\\\ 0.41 & =\\frac{0.0171}{0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(D|{F}_{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.41(0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2})) & =0.0171 \\\\ 0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{0.0171}{0.41} \\\\ 0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{171}{4100} \\\\ 0.45P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{171}{4100}-0.0235 \\\\ P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{0.45}(\\frac{171}{4100}-0.0235) \\\\ P(D|{F}_{2}) & ≈0.040\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "Let $A$ be the event that a light bulb is of type A, $D$ be the event that a light bulb is defective, and ${F}_{1},{F}_{2},{F}_{3}$ be the event that a light bulb is produced in factory $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({F}_{1})=0.45 &  & P(D|{F}_{1})=0.05 \\\\  & P({F}_{2})=0.45 \\\\  & P({F}_{3})=0.1 &  & P(D|{F}_{3})=0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${F}_{1}⊔{F}_{2}⊔{F}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({F}_{1})+P({F}_{2})+P({F}_{3})=0.45+0.45+0.1=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $57\\%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.57.\n$$\n\n- Since $3\\%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.03.\n$$\n\n- Since $41\\%$ of defective light bulbs are of type A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A|D)=0.41\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen light bulb from factory $2$ is defective, which is represented as $P(D|{F}_{2}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|D) & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D)} \\\\ P(A|D) & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D|{F}_{1})P({F}_{1})+P(D|{F}_{2})P({F}_{2})+P(D|{F}_{3})P({F}_{3})} \\\\ 0.41 & =\\frac{(0.03)(0.57)}{(0.05)(0.45)+P(D|{F}_{2})(0.45)+(0.01)(0.1)} \\\\ 0.41 & =\\frac{0.0171}{0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(D|{F}_{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.41(0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2})) & =0.0171 \\\\ 0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{0.0171}{0.41} \\\\ 0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{171}{4100} \\\\ 0.45P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{171}{4100}-0.0235 \\\\ P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{0.45}(\\frac{171}{4100}-0.0235) \\\\ P(D|{F}_{2}) & ≈0.040\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186493917 :question/problem "The output voltage of a particular model of laptop AC adapters is $19$ volts on average. However, $1%$ of adapters have a voltage greater than $20$ volts. Assuming that the output voltage is normally distributed, find the percent of AC adapters with a voltage greater than $19.3$ volts, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.900\\,$ | $\\,0.950\\,$ | $\\,0.975\\,$ | $\\,0.990\\,$ | $\\,0.995\\,$ | $\\,0.999\\,$ | $\\,0.9995\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ |" "The output voltage of a particular model of